From 4b63958d241e25868446be1339c5921449061aa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timur Ravilov <93673987+t0b1kent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 04:57:36 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: MACRUNNER_FEX_ALIAS_VIEWS - executable views of
 non-image sections (lazy discovery + validate-only intervals)

An executable view of an ordinary (non-image) section - the RX half of a W^X double mapping (.NET 7+, V8) - reaches none of the hooks
that fill XIntervals: NotifyMemoryAlloc/NotifyMemoryProtect see only allocations and protection changes, HandleImageMap only images,
and Wine's notify_map_view_of_section drops non-image sections. QueryExecutableRange answers "not executable" and the guest gets
"page fault on execute access" in every validation mode. Even when the view is registered (VirtualProtect), it is not writable
code for FEX, so a write through the RW view is never validated and runs stale code unless the guest flushes.

MACRUNNER_FEX_ALIAS_VIEWS=1 (default 0 = previous code):
- layer B: on a QueryExecutableRange miss ask NtQueryVirtualMemory; MEM_COMMIT + MEM_MAPPED + executable protection -> the run of pages
  goes into XIntervals and the query is repeated once. No invalidation: the query runs from the decoder under the SHARED
  CodeInvalidationMutex and InvalidateIntervalInternal takes it exclusively (self-deadlock). Safe because the miss is the first fetch.
- layer C (MACRUNNER_FEX_ALIAS_VALIDATE=0 switches it off): such views are validate-only. AliasIntervals answers Writable = NoTrap =
  true, pages are NOT protected (a write through another view is invisible to this view's protection). HandleMemoryProtectionNotification
  marks them too (the VirtualProtect path), and every place that drops X/RWX intervals drops the alias intervals.
Answers for neighbouring addresses of the same X interval stop in front of an alias interval, like in front of the next RWX interval,
and an RWX answer starts at the queried address when an alias interval lies behind it inside the same RWX interval (the decoder caches
the answer and reuses it for neighbouring instructions, so it must not cover addresses of another class; checked against a brute-force
page model on 64720 random addresses, gate off == previous code on 4000 random layouts).

Gate off: QueryExecutableRange returns exactly what it returned before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
